WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.574 --> 00:00:05.227
- Good morning, Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:05.227 --> 00:00:07.092
I started the Belief Life series

00:00:07.092 --> 00:00:09.908
to try to round out our knowledge gaps

00:00:09.908 --> 00:00:12.914
as entrepreneurs and not
only focus on business stuff,

00:00:12.914 --> 00:00:16.498
but give us some useful tools
to help us in our daily lives.

00:00:16.498 --> 00:00:18.386
And so this weekend we're going to focus

00:00:18.386 --> 00:00:21.580
on cooking tips for beginners,

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.144
something that I probably need
to pay a lot of attention to.

00:00:25.144 --> 00:00:26.394
Hope you enjoy.

00:00:44.285 --> 00:00:45.637
- Cooking is a life skill.

00:00:45.637 --> 00:00:47.104
For me, it's fundamentally important.

00:00:47.104 --> 00:00:50.221
It's just as crucial as keeping fit.

00:00:50.221 --> 00:00:53.495
Because Latin, history, geography,

00:00:53.495 --> 00:00:55.594
no disrespect, but if you're
not going to teach that

00:00:55.594 --> 00:00:58.128
for the rest of your life,
it doesn't come into play.

00:00:58.128 --> 00:00:59.129
Cooking does.

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:01.035
Three times a day, seven days a week,

00:01:01.035 --> 00:01:02.033
for the rest of your life.

00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:03.033
You need to eat.

00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:04.220
So we don't cook three meals a day,

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.818
but there's one meal across that day

00:01:05.818 --> 00:01:08.225
that needs to be absolutely you.

00:01:08.225 --> 00:01:12.586
On a plate, done properly,
healthily cooked,

00:01:12.586 --> 00:01:13.673
and sets you up.

00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:14.670
'Cause you are what you eat.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.789
So we can never underestimate
the importance of food,

00:01:16.789 --> 00:01:18.789
because that's the fuel.

00:01:22.857 --> 00:01:23.933
- Okay, lovely people.

00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:27.033
Knife skills: really, really important.

00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:29.346
Life is short, you normally
eat three times a day,

00:01:29.346 --> 00:01:31.135
you probably cook a lot
of meals in your life,

00:01:31.135 --> 00:01:32.531
and sit there, clanking around,

00:01:32.531 --> 00:01:33.973
taking lumps of your body off,

00:01:33.973 --> 00:01:35.753
and like just being slow and rubbish.

00:01:35.753 --> 00:01:36.586
Not good.

00:01:36.586 --> 00:01:38.521
So let's get you good at it.

00:01:38.521 --> 00:01:40.458
so there's four things
in front of me right now:

00:01:40.458 --> 00:01:43.341
a small paring knife,
nice little darling here.

00:01:43.341 --> 00:01:45.609
Great for sort of little preparations,

00:01:45.609 --> 00:01:47.769
nipping things, slicing herbs.

00:01:47.769 --> 00:01:50.662
A really good, all-around knife, right?

00:01:50.662 --> 00:01:53.937
Everyone needs one, an essential, okay?

00:01:53.937 --> 00:01:56.175
Now, not so really an all-around knife,

00:01:56.175 --> 00:01:58.508
but this is a carving knife.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:02.162
You can use it for
carving meats and stuff.

00:02:02.162 --> 00:02:03.530
It does leave a little bit of a line,

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:04.984
but it doesn't really bother me.

00:02:04.984 --> 00:02:06.612
But most importantly
you've got the teeth in it,

00:02:06.612 --> 00:02:09.796
the serrated sort of jagged dagger edges.

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:13.437
It's a really good sort of
all-around carving knife there.

00:02:13.437 --> 00:02:14.769
And then the knife that
you're going to use

00:02:14.769 --> 00:02:16.684
90% of the time, right?

00:02:16.684 --> 00:02:19.758
An eight to 10-inch chef's knife.

00:02:19.758 --> 00:02:23.447
Absolutely brilliant,
well-balanced, nice grip.

00:02:23.447 --> 00:02:26.634
You've got that thick kind of base here,

00:02:26.634 --> 00:02:28.871
and that base is also thick here.

00:02:28.871 --> 00:02:32.511
So with little chicken legs,
you can just nick 'em off,

00:02:32.511 --> 00:02:35.912
and just literally break bones
in bits and pieces like that.

00:02:35.912 --> 00:02:39.398
You've got that nice thick edge here.

00:02:39.398 --> 00:02:40.726
So that's what you need.

00:02:40.726 --> 00:02:41.924
We've got the sharpener here.

00:02:41.924 --> 00:02:43.818
Every time you use a knife:

00:02:43.818 --> 00:02:44.985
bottom to tip.

00:02:45.937 --> 00:02:47.104
Bottom to tip.

00:02:48.838 --> 00:02:50.991
Just do it once on each side.

00:02:50.991 --> 00:02:53.127
Okay, there's three methods of chopping

00:02:53.127 --> 00:02:55.308
that I suggest you learn, okay?

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:57.572
And that will get you 99% of stuff.

00:02:57.572 --> 00:03:00.988
This first method I'm going
to teach you, the cross chop,

00:03:00.988 --> 00:03:02.384
is brilliant.

00:03:02.384 --> 00:03:05.765
Because hands and fingers are like this,

00:03:05.765 --> 00:03:08.993
there's no weight on the knife,

00:03:08.993 --> 00:03:11.321
and that is very, very easy.

00:03:11.321 --> 00:03:12.803
Right, it's safe.

00:03:12.803 --> 00:03:14.015
No fingers, no limbs,

00:03:14.015 --> 00:03:16.886
no body parts anywhere near this knife.

00:03:16.886 --> 00:03:20.484
And then when you're chopping things...

00:03:20.484 --> 00:03:22.668
Use it almost like a dust pan and brush.

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:24.836
Let's, for instance, hack up this onion.

00:03:24.836 --> 00:03:26.446
Let's just cross chop.

00:03:26.446 --> 00:03:28.781
So it's bad, it's hacking it, right?

00:03:28.781 --> 00:03:30.340
But then you keep going through it,

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.357
and then it's almost
like a food processor.

00:03:33.357 --> 00:03:35.688
It's going to get fine eventually, right?

00:03:35.688 --> 00:03:37.447
But it's falling off,
so then you sweep it up

00:03:37.447 --> 00:03:39.072
and you put it in the middle.

00:03:39.072 --> 00:03:41.585
This is quite a slow way of doing this,

00:03:41.585 --> 00:03:45.025
but it is efficient and it is safe.

00:03:45.025 --> 00:03:46.569
You might see people doing that.

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:48.509
Never, ever, ever do that.

00:03:48.509 --> 00:03:50.742
Always fingers like that.

00:03:50.742 --> 00:03:52.110
I've seen people take their fingertips off

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.662
if they do that, okay?

00:03:53.662 --> 00:03:55.030
Don't do it.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.363
Hands like that.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:03:59.615
So let's just get rid of that.

00:03:59.615 --> 00:04:02.172
So I'm going to go
straight into tap chopping.

00:04:02.172 --> 00:04:05.172
The idea is that I put my thumb here

00:04:06.436 --> 00:04:08.286
and I put my fingers here.

00:04:08.286 --> 00:04:09.490
And my fingers are tucked in

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:12.003
and I pull my fingers as I'm
cutting all the way down.

00:04:12.003 --> 00:04:14.432
And then I redo it and
all the way down, okay?

00:04:14.432 --> 00:04:16.356
If you forget your thumb and it comes out,

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:17.189
you're going to cut that off, as well.

00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:19.137
And I have, we all have.

00:04:19.137 --> 00:04:23.135
But I didn't have anyone
telling me how to do it.

00:04:23.135 --> 00:04:23.968
So...

00:04:25.426 --> 00:04:27.673
The top of the knife
is feeling that finger,

00:04:27.673 --> 00:04:30.281
but not obviously, it knows where it is.

00:04:30.281 --> 00:04:31.869
I'm not bringing it up beyond here.

00:04:31.869 --> 00:04:33.740
You never, ever bring this blade

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.190
beyond the point where
your finger goes in, okay?

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.988
So never higher than there, right?

00:04:40.988 --> 00:04:41.821
Slowly.

00:04:44.072 --> 00:04:45.489
As I'm slicing...

00:04:50.144 --> 00:04:53.881
Now, you're going to
go slow to start with,

00:04:53.881 --> 00:04:57.464
and then you can start
to pick up the speed

00:04:59.127 --> 00:05:03.345
to the point where it
doesn't matter anymore, okay?

00:05:03.345 --> 00:05:04.887
Now, the reality is that,

00:05:04.887 --> 00:05:07.560
'cause I know where my fingers are,

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.153
'cause I know what's
happening 'til this point,

00:05:10.153 --> 00:05:11.557
at this point I've got wobble again.

00:05:11.557 --> 00:05:13.434
See, 'cause I'm getting towards
the end of the courgette.

00:05:13.434 --> 00:05:15.100
What do you when you got wobble?

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.637
On a flat surface, wobbling?

00:05:16.637 --> 00:05:18.192
No, alright.

00:05:18.192 --> 00:05:19.887
And then you can chop again, until then,

00:05:19.887 --> 00:05:20.818
and you're wobbling again.

00:05:20.818 --> 00:05:22.104
Then you go to flat surface.

00:05:22.104 --> 00:05:22.937
Right?

00:05:23.996 --> 00:05:27.329
So chopping is all about non-wobbly veg.

00:05:28.743 --> 00:05:30.735
You're in control of that.

00:05:30.735 --> 00:05:33.874
So let's just have a
little go on this chili.

00:05:33.874 --> 00:05:34.765
That's a rock chopping.

00:05:34.765 --> 00:05:37.089
chili's perfect for rock chopping.

00:05:37.089 --> 00:05:40.302
Let's change the angle,
let's show you it like this.

00:05:40.302 --> 00:05:41.773
If you look at this chili now,

00:05:41.773 --> 00:05:44.933
it doesn't matter if
I'm going slow or fast.

00:05:44.933 --> 00:05:46.148
No, it's fingers in.

00:05:46.148 --> 00:05:47.996
From pulling the fingers towards it.

00:05:47.996 --> 00:05:49.217
That little edge of my finger

00:05:49.217 --> 00:05:50.631
controlling where the blade's going.

00:05:50.631 --> 00:05:53.184
At no point does that blade
ever go near a finger.

00:05:53.184 --> 00:05:54.353
It's vulnerable now,

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:57.310
so then we're much, much more careful.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:58.460
And then we finish.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.185
That's a chili.

00:06:00.185 --> 00:06:01.529
Practice makes perfect.

00:06:01.529 --> 00:06:02.963
If your fingers are always out of the way,

00:06:02.963 --> 00:06:04.880
you can't chop 'em off.

00:06:05.958 --> 00:06:07.935
It's like learning to write,
it's like learning to talk,

00:06:07.935 --> 00:06:10.032
it's like learning to ride a bike.

00:06:10.032 --> 00:06:12.286
It makes your cooking so much quicker.

00:06:12.286 --> 00:06:14.006
And for the show-offs out there,

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:14.839
you can style it out.

00:06:14.839 --> 00:06:15.672
Good luck.

00:06:15.672 --> 00:06:16.700
- Make lists.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:18.671
What ingredients do I
need for this recipe?

00:06:18.671 --> 00:06:20.488
Make a coherent and comprehensive list.

00:06:20.488 --> 00:06:21.321
(bell rings once)

00:06:21.321 --> 00:06:23.667
Mise en place, meaning organize your stuff

00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:25.318
so that it's laid out in front of you.

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:26.796
All your ingredients.

00:06:26.796 --> 00:06:28.831
The most advanced
preparation you could have

00:06:28.831 --> 00:06:30.513
under the circumstances

00:06:30.513 --> 00:06:32.214
before your guests arrive.

00:06:32.214 --> 00:06:34.424
You don't want your guests sitting there,

00:06:34.424 --> 00:06:36.685
drinking all your liquor
and getting ugly drunk

00:06:36.685 --> 00:06:39.166
while you're around in the kitchen.

00:06:39.166 --> 00:06:41.655
- [Martha] When measuring
sticky ingredients like honey,

00:06:41.655 --> 00:06:43.069
spray a glass measuring cup

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:45.598
with a non-stick cooking spray like Pam

00:06:45.598 --> 00:06:48.725
to prevent sticking and
allow for easy cleanup.

00:06:48.725 --> 00:06:49.842
Then fill.

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:51.020
And when you pour it out,

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:52.205
you'll see that it doesn't stick

00:06:52.205 --> 00:06:54.514
to the sides of the measuring cup.

00:06:54.514 --> 00:06:57.277
When measuring liquids, use a
spouted glass measuring cup.

00:06:57.277 --> 00:06:59.599
Fill with the liquid on a flat surface.

00:06:59.599 --> 00:07:02.505
And then bend down to read at eye level.

00:07:02.505 --> 00:07:03.884
When measuring dry ingredients,

00:07:03.884 --> 00:07:06.615
be sure to use a dry measuring cup.

00:07:06.615 --> 00:07:08.511
Dip the measuring cup into a canister,

00:07:08.511 --> 00:07:10.097
this one has granulated sugar,

00:07:10.097 --> 00:07:14.018
and sweep away the excess
with a straight-sided utensil.

00:07:14.018 --> 00:07:16.466
When using a scale to
measure items like tomatoes,

00:07:16.466 --> 00:07:21.200
place a bowl on the scale and
press the zero or Tare button,

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.318
which will set the scale to zero.

00:07:23.318 --> 00:07:24.869
Then add the ingredients you're weighing

00:07:24.869 --> 00:07:27.826
and you'll see you have
an accurate measure.

00:07:27.826 --> 00:07:28.659
- Hello, lovely people.

00:07:28.659 --> 00:07:30.889
Have you ever got a little
piece of eggshell in your eggs

00:07:30.889 --> 00:07:31.722
when you're cracking 'em?

00:07:31.722 --> 00:07:34.057
Well, let me show you the
quickest and easiest way

00:07:34.057 --> 00:07:35.219
to get rid of it.

00:07:35.219 --> 00:07:36.245
We've all done it.

00:07:36.245 --> 00:07:38.027
We're making an omelet,
we're making a cake,

00:07:38.027 --> 00:07:39.698
and we're cracking our eggs,

00:07:39.698 --> 00:07:43.674
and then a little bit of
random shell just accidentally,

00:07:43.674 --> 00:07:45.883
oh, falls into the egg.

00:07:45.883 --> 00:07:47.733
Now the thing is the natural instinct

00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:49.945
is to get in there with your fingers.

00:07:49.945 --> 00:07:52.722
But if you try and get
it, it always runs away

00:07:52.722 --> 00:07:53.555
and it's kind of annoying.

00:07:53.555 --> 00:07:55.649
The easiest way to do
it, believe it or not,

00:07:55.649 --> 00:07:58.618
is to go back to our friend Mr. Shell,

00:07:58.618 --> 00:08:00.429
and you can mop it up easily,

00:08:00.429 --> 00:08:04.437
get those annoying bits of
shell every single time.

00:08:04.437 --> 00:08:05.549
Happy days.

00:08:05.549 --> 00:08:08.913
Let me show you how to remove
the skin easily and fast

00:08:08.913 --> 00:08:10.033
from an avocado.

00:08:10.033 --> 00:08:12.263
Get a regular spoon like this,

00:08:12.263 --> 00:08:16.096
go in here, push the
spoon right onto the skin

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:19.101
and push it against the cup of your hand

00:08:19.101 --> 00:08:21.101
and simply knock it out.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:25.627
Works every single time
really, really good.

00:08:27.121 --> 00:08:28.445
Let me show you some really easy ways

00:08:28.445 --> 00:08:30.497
to chop and slice your garlic.

00:08:30.497 --> 00:08:33.386
We can slice it, we want to
get our thumb out of the way.

00:08:33.386 --> 00:08:36.138
We want our fingers to go
over the garlic like this.

00:08:36.138 --> 00:08:38.750
And just do that little
tap chopping method.

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:40.928
We can just continue to chop through this

00:08:40.928 --> 00:08:42.455
to make chopped garlic.

00:08:42.455 --> 00:08:44.417
And that's the cross
chopping method, very safe.

00:08:44.417 --> 00:08:46.132
Just grab your garlic clove like this.

00:08:46.132 --> 00:08:47.448
Just put it on a flat edge.

00:08:47.448 --> 00:08:49.057
Just go about millimeter slices.

00:08:49.057 --> 00:08:50.984
Not quite to the end like that,

00:08:50.984 --> 00:08:53.469
and then flip it over
and do exactly the same.

00:08:53.469 --> 00:08:54.533
And then you can put it on its side

00:08:54.533 --> 00:08:56.804
and then you can literally just slice.

00:08:56.804 --> 00:08:59.536
That's rock chopping method.

00:08:59.536 --> 00:09:01.033
Let me show you how to take the stone

00:09:01.033 --> 00:09:03.138
out of an avocado safely.

00:09:03.138 --> 00:09:05.562
So just remove a little
bit of stem from the end.

00:09:05.562 --> 00:09:07.692
It might look like nothing,
but that can send your knife

00:09:07.692 --> 00:09:10.412
in a totally different direction,
which is really dangerous.

00:09:10.412 --> 00:09:12.756
And then put the avocado
down, very importantly.

00:09:12.756 --> 00:09:16.758
And then carefully but slowly
cut using no weight or power

00:09:16.758 --> 00:09:18.402
down to the stone in the middle

00:09:18.402 --> 00:09:22.569
and slowly rotate the avocado
so it just gently cuts around.

00:09:23.447 --> 00:09:24.280
Twist it,

00:09:25.755 --> 00:09:28.172
and then tap it on the board.

00:09:30.153 --> 00:09:31.570
And off it comes.

00:09:33.386 --> 00:09:35.256
It's so important to have a sharp knife.

00:09:35.256 --> 00:09:37.262
Let me show you how to sharpen it.

00:09:37.262 --> 00:09:39.874
First off, you need to get a steel.

00:09:39.874 --> 00:09:42.389
Start with the heel of the
knife at a slight angle,

00:09:42.389 --> 00:09:44.722
and we go evenly to the tip.

00:09:46.746 --> 00:09:48.008
And we do the other side.

00:09:48.008 --> 00:09:49.837
So one, two.

00:09:49.837 --> 00:09:51.938
Really, that's all you should need to do,

00:09:51.938 --> 00:09:54.274
and that's called knife maintenance.

00:09:54.274 --> 00:09:57.016
Let me show you two
ways to peel your ginger

00:09:57.016 --> 00:09:58.704
in the most efficient way.

00:09:58.704 --> 00:10:01.631
The most efficient way
to peel a piece of ginger

00:10:01.631 --> 00:10:04.844
is simply to use a little
spoon and scrape it,

00:10:04.844 --> 00:10:06.252
just like that.

00:10:06.252 --> 00:10:09.709
It barely peels just the brown skin off.

00:10:09.709 --> 00:10:13.252
Let me show you another
way to peel ginger.

00:10:13.252 --> 00:10:16.354
You can make the most
fantastic ginger teas

00:10:16.354 --> 00:10:17.736
with a little teaspoon of honey.

00:10:17.736 --> 00:10:19.133
One of the most important things to do

00:10:19.133 --> 00:10:20.510
when you're cooking in the kitchen

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.193
is secure your chopping board.

00:10:22.193 --> 00:10:23.508
Let me show you how.

00:10:23.508 --> 00:10:26.009
It's the simplest thing
on the planet to do,

00:10:26.009 --> 00:10:28.568
but just get yourself a little tea towel,

00:10:28.568 --> 00:10:30.053
place it down like that,

00:10:30.053 --> 00:10:32.958
pour some water on like this,

00:10:32.958 --> 00:10:35.735
and it will kind of
shape to whatever shape

00:10:35.735 --> 00:10:38.152
the surface and the board is.

00:10:39.678 --> 00:10:41.707
I'm going to show you how
to cut and prepare a mango.

00:10:41.707 --> 00:10:43.460
Put it to its flat side like this.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:47.541
I put my knife in, and you're
going to hit the oval-shaped pip.

00:10:47.541 --> 00:10:51.774
Feather out the knife so
it goes around the pip.

00:10:51.774 --> 00:10:53.638
Take this lobe into three.

00:10:53.638 --> 00:10:55.750
Now once you've done
that you got two choices:

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:57.467
you can either put the tip of your knife

00:10:57.467 --> 00:10:59.307
just under the skin,

00:10:59.307 --> 00:11:02.959
and then just gently roll
the knife over the skin

00:11:02.959 --> 00:11:04.629
and just remove that skin like that.

00:11:04.629 --> 00:11:07.993
Or get yourself a sturdy
glass, nothing too fragile.

00:11:07.993 --> 00:11:11.720
Literally push it, it takes
off the flesh like this,

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:13.025
keeping it in one place.

00:11:13.025 --> 00:11:15.185
You are left with the flesh here.

00:11:15.185 --> 00:11:16.167
Take the end off.

00:11:16.167 --> 00:11:17.898
Keep your knife almost in the same place,

00:11:17.898 --> 00:11:20.043
and then just twiddle and roll the pip,

00:11:20.043 --> 00:11:22.352
and that is for the chef.

00:11:22.352 --> 00:11:23.846
Let me show you the easiest way

00:11:23.846 --> 00:11:26.607
to take an egg yolk away
from the egg whites.

00:11:26.607 --> 00:11:31.015
Now, one way is to bounce the
yolk backwards and forwards

00:11:31.015 --> 00:11:32.075
from one shell to the other.

00:11:32.075 --> 00:11:33.110
That's the classic.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:35.925
But the thing is, sometimes
you kind of pierce the yolk

00:11:35.925 --> 00:11:37.249
and then it goes in your egg whites.

00:11:37.249 --> 00:11:39.184
That means your meringues
are not going to work,

00:11:39.184 --> 00:11:41.022
and that means you've lost your yolks.

00:11:41.022 --> 00:11:44.847
The other way to do it:
crack it into your hand

00:11:44.847 --> 00:11:48.883
and let it slide through
until you've got your yolk.

00:11:48.883 --> 00:11:52.806
And then there's one more
flamboyant way to do it.

00:11:52.806 --> 00:11:55.514
Get yourself a clean, empty bottle,

00:11:55.514 --> 00:11:57.559
take the top off,

00:11:57.559 --> 00:11:59.678
squeeze a little bit of the air out,

00:11:59.678 --> 00:12:01.428
and suck up the yolk.

00:12:04.145 --> 00:12:06.468
I'm going to show you how you
can get those beautiful capsules

00:12:06.468 --> 00:12:07.607
out of the pomegranate.

00:12:07.607 --> 00:12:09.807
Buy yourself a beautiful
pomegranate, cut it open,

00:12:09.807 --> 00:12:11.556
put your fingers open like that,

00:12:11.556 --> 00:12:14.827
hold the half over a bowl
or a salad or any dish,

00:12:14.827 --> 00:12:16.370
and just give it a good spanking,

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:18.287
which most people love.

00:12:19.836 --> 00:12:23.553
And they literally just tumble out.

00:12:23.553 --> 00:12:24.984
- When you're getting corn off the cob,

00:12:24.984 --> 00:12:28.073
put a small bowl upside
down in a large bowl.

00:12:28.073 --> 00:12:31.429
Take a good sharp knife
and just spin the corn,

00:12:31.429 --> 00:12:34.011
and the corn collects in the larger bowl.

00:12:34.011 --> 00:12:36.060
(applause)

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:37.178
This is very clever.

00:12:37.178 --> 00:12:38.465
This is a cooling rack

00:12:38.465 --> 00:12:41.278
for when you're baking your
holiday cookies and all that.

00:12:41.278 --> 00:12:43.492
But I use it to make a
big batch of guacamole.

00:12:43.492 --> 00:12:44.493
Check it out.

00:12:44.493 --> 00:12:46.993
Your charred or fresh avocado,

00:12:47.873 --> 00:12:48.706
squish...

00:12:48.706 --> 00:12:49.891
- [Audience] Ahh!

00:12:49.891 --> 00:12:50.724
- Done!

00:12:50.724 --> 00:12:53.488
(applause)

00:12:53.488 --> 00:12:54.488
Hot, right?

00:12:54.488 --> 00:12:55.405
That's hot.

00:12:56.655 --> 00:12:59.349
Hey guys, the next time you have to slice

00:12:59.349 --> 00:13:01.070
a lot of cherry tomatoes,

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:03.249
don't go one by one with a paring knife.

00:13:03.249 --> 00:13:05.324
Take out some deli lids.

00:13:05.324 --> 00:13:08.460
Fill the bottom lid with
a lot of cherry tomatoes,

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:12.361
put an extra lid up on top, make one cut,

00:13:12.361 --> 00:13:14.881
and slice all those tomatoes at one time.

00:13:14.881 --> 00:13:18.154
Works for grapes, too, if
you're feeding little ones.

00:13:18.154 --> 00:13:20.063
- And then we'll mix the dry...

00:13:20.063 --> 00:13:21.743
(applause)

00:13:21.743 --> 00:13:23.380
Then take an ice cream scooper--

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:24.706
- Isn't that awesome?

00:13:24.706 --> 00:13:26.877
When you're making cupcakes or muffins...

00:13:26.877 --> 00:13:27.710
- Amazing!

00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:30.332
- You get identical
portions for everybody.

00:13:30.332 --> 00:13:32.759
You don't end up in the end
with some of them bubbling over

00:13:32.759 --> 00:13:34.282
and some being too short.

00:13:34.282 --> 00:13:35.115
It's awesome.

00:13:35.115 --> 00:13:36.085
- I'm stealing that idea for sure.

00:13:36.085 --> 00:13:37.612
- [Rachel] Yeah, it's a good one.

00:13:37.612 --> 00:13:39.125
- Look how perfectly that goes in.

00:13:39.125 --> 00:13:40.483
It's ingenious.
- Isn't that awesome?

00:13:40.483 --> 00:13:41.882
It's really fun and easy,

00:13:41.882 --> 00:13:44.850
and then it comes out
exactly even all the time.

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:45.683
I love it.
- Amazing.

00:13:45.683 --> 00:13:46.516
- It's a good tip.

00:13:46.516 --> 00:13:49.096
If you don't have one of
these little juicers at home,

00:13:49.096 --> 00:13:52.386
a great tip is to just
squeeze the lemon cut side up.

00:13:52.386 --> 00:13:54.227
The seeds stay with the
lemon and don't fall down

00:13:54.227 --> 00:13:57.002
into whatever you're cooking or preparing.

00:13:57.002 --> 00:13:59.256
(applause)

00:13:59.256 --> 00:14:01.464
When it comes to freezing food,

00:14:01.464 --> 00:14:03.939
remember, flat is best.

00:14:03.939 --> 00:14:06.721
Just take a durable
freezer bag for stability.

00:14:06.721 --> 00:14:07.981
If you're dealing with liquids,

00:14:07.981 --> 00:14:11.219
place it in a bowl or a large
measuring cup like this,

00:14:11.219 --> 00:14:14.881
then fill your hot soup
or stew or sauce in there,

00:14:14.881 --> 00:14:16.202
let it cool completely,

00:14:16.202 --> 00:14:18.271
and store it nice and flat.

00:14:18.271 --> 00:14:22.246
Once it's frozen you could
stack these up like 10, 12 high.

00:14:22.246 --> 00:14:24.843
Guys, when you bring home
beautiful fresh basil

00:14:24.843 --> 00:14:25.843
from the grocery store,

00:14:25.843 --> 00:14:28.430
do not head for your fridge.

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:30.713
Basil hates the cold.

00:14:30.713 --> 00:14:32.332
Doesn't like water much, either.

00:14:32.332 --> 00:14:34.404
You need to treat it
like fresh-cut flowers:

00:14:34.404 --> 00:14:36.523
trim the bottoms, put it in clean water,

00:14:36.523 --> 00:14:38.092
change that water every couple of days,

00:14:38.092 --> 00:14:39.767
leave it on the counter,
not in the fridge,

00:14:39.767 --> 00:14:44.402
and then cover it with a
plastic food storage bag.

00:14:44.402 --> 00:14:45.569
This creates a little greenhouse,

00:14:45.569 --> 00:14:47.605
and the basil will not only not go bad,

00:14:47.605 --> 00:14:49.414
it'll continue to grow.

00:14:49.414 --> 00:14:52.310
You'll use every last leaf.

00:14:52.310 --> 00:14:54.098
- Have reasonable
expectations of yourself.

00:14:54.098 --> 00:14:56.806
When professionals learn how
to make a hollandaise sauce,

00:14:56.806 --> 00:14:59.033
they screw it up the first time,

00:14:59.033 --> 00:15:00.710
and probably the second and the third.

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:02.369
So cut yourself some slack.

00:15:02.369 --> 00:15:05.024
Understand that repetition,
repetition, repetition,

00:15:05.024 --> 00:15:08.818
that some dishes take repeated doing

00:15:08.818 --> 00:15:10.819
'til you get them right.

00:15:10.819 --> 00:15:14.216
If you're bringing over
your new girlfriend's family

00:15:14.216 --> 00:15:16.273
for the first time and you
want to make a good impression,

00:15:16.273 --> 00:15:18.866
maybe this is not the day to do something

00:15:18.866 --> 00:15:20.269
you've never done before.

00:15:20.269 --> 00:15:21.838
Do something you're very familiar with,

00:15:21.838 --> 00:15:24.391
you know you do well,
you're comfortable with.

00:15:24.391 --> 00:15:27.339
And if you're trying to do
something completely new,

00:15:27.339 --> 00:15:30.287
invite only people over
who've seen you naked already.

00:15:30.287 --> 00:15:32.199
- And that is, I think,
this is one of this

00:15:32.199 --> 00:15:35.782
awful American syndrome
of fear of failure.

00:15:38.582 --> 00:15:41.686
And if you're going to have
a sense of fear of failure,

00:15:41.686 --> 00:15:43.287
you're just never going
to learn how to cook,

00:15:43.287 --> 00:15:45.666
because cooking is...

00:15:45.666 --> 00:15:48.385
Well, lots of it is one
failure after another,

00:15:48.385 --> 00:15:50.898
and that's how you finally learn.

00:15:50.898 --> 00:15:54.407
For instance, you've got to
develop what the French call...

00:15:54.407 --> 00:15:56.147
(says phrase in French)

00:15:56.147 --> 00:15:57.906
I don't care what happens.

00:15:57.906 --> 00:16:02.132
I can fall, and omelets
can go all over the stove.

00:16:02.132 --> 00:16:04.799
I'm going to learn, I shall overcome,

00:16:04.799 --> 00:16:06.254
that sort of women's liberation.

00:16:06.254 --> 00:16:08.435
And I mean, everything like that,

00:16:08.435 --> 00:16:10.712
if you're not going to be ready to fail,

00:16:10.712 --> 00:16:12.585
you're not going to learn how to cook.

00:16:12.585 --> 00:16:16.752
(chuckling) That's what that
little lecture was all about.

00:16:17.887 --> 00:16:20.810
For one thing, nobody knows
exactly what you intend,

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:22.549
so you can serve them
one of your failures,

00:16:22.549 --> 00:16:25.244
and they maybe think
that's what you intended.

00:16:25.244 --> 00:16:26.334
- Biggest thing about cooking:

00:16:26.334 --> 00:16:27.167
when you're starting,

00:16:27.167 --> 00:16:30.132
actually, anyone out there
that's just starting how to cook,

00:16:30.132 --> 00:16:31.305
I'm actually jealous of you,

00:16:31.305 --> 00:16:33.709
because if I could go back in time

00:16:33.709 --> 00:16:35.415
and start learning how to cook now,

00:16:35.415 --> 00:16:38.432
I'd be a lot more mindful and
I wouldn't develop bad habits.

00:16:38.432 --> 00:16:40.180
And one of the biggest
habits that cooks do

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:42.871
is they rush and they end
up cutting themselves.

00:16:42.871 --> 00:16:44.092
If you look at the potato,

00:16:44.092 --> 00:16:45.726
and you're like, oh,
this is kind of wobbly,

00:16:45.726 --> 00:16:47.556
know that there's a chance
your knife's going to slip.

00:16:47.556 --> 00:16:49.307
So when you're cutting,
make sure it's firm.

00:16:49.307 --> 00:16:50.890
Just go one by one.

00:16:51.743 --> 00:16:54.506
If you're watching yourself
and you're paying attention,

00:16:54.506 --> 00:16:55.836
just like learning anything,

00:16:55.836 --> 00:16:58.605
you're not going to develop
those bad habits of rushing.

00:16:58.605 --> 00:17:00.069
And I know cooking, some people are like,

00:17:00.069 --> 00:17:02.148
oh, I don't have time to
do it, it takes too long,

00:17:02.148 --> 00:17:03.396
it's too hard.

00:17:03.396 --> 00:17:05.077
It's all a bunch of bullcrap.

00:17:05.077 --> 00:17:06.797
All you have to do is
just have some patience

00:17:06.797 --> 00:17:08.285
and just practice.

00:17:08.285 --> 00:17:09.118
When you're cooking,

00:17:09.118 --> 00:17:12.424
not everything has to be cut the same way.

00:17:12.424 --> 00:17:16.235
If you're a chef, if you're
going to culinary school,

00:17:16.235 --> 00:17:17.479
if that's your thing

00:17:17.479 --> 00:17:19.589
and you have to cut everything
perfectly, that's fine.

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:21.377
You know, in a lot of restaurants

00:17:21.377 --> 00:17:23.085
they're very demanding of that.

00:17:23.085 --> 00:17:24.648
But you know, if you're just having fun

00:17:24.648 --> 00:17:25.882
and you're cooking for friends,

00:17:25.882 --> 00:17:28.644
things don't have to be that consistent.

00:17:28.644 --> 00:17:30.128
Just to be, you know,
invite some friends over,

00:17:30.128 --> 00:17:31.137
have a good time.

00:17:31.137 --> 00:17:34.125
Cook for some people, or
just cook for yourself.

00:17:34.125 --> 00:17:35.592
And as long as you're having fun with it,

00:17:35.592 --> 00:17:37.498
don't feel like it has to be intimidating.

00:17:37.498 --> 00:17:39.963
The first times you cook for people,

00:17:39.963 --> 00:17:41.645
try the recipe yourself a couple times

00:17:41.645 --> 00:17:43.516
and just see how it turns out.

00:17:43.516 --> 00:17:45.584
And if it's not good,
if your friends eat it

00:17:45.584 --> 00:17:46.714
and they're like, it's not that great,

00:17:46.714 --> 00:17:48.837
or maybe it just needs more seasoning,

00:17:48.837 --> 00:17:50.166
don't feel like you have to end.

00:17:50.166 --> 00:17:53.333
If I taste this right now, I'm like...

00:17:56.025 --> 00:17:58.872
It just doesn't have that boom,

00:17:58.872 --> 00:18:02.630
I'm sitting at the table,
I don't just accept defeat.

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:05.532
I go to the cabinet and I grab the salt,

00:18:05.532 --> 00:18:07.994
I squeeze some salt in there

00:18:07.994 --> 00:18:09.494
and mix it around.

00:18:11.389 --> 00:18:15.366
And I'll be damned if I taste it again

00:18:15.366 --> 00:18:17.616
to make sure that's better.

00:18:18.721 --> 00:18:19.802
There you go.

00:18:19.802 --> 00:18:21.509
It's actually a big difference.

00:18:21.509 --> 00:18:23.063
And if it's still not good,

00:18:23.063 --> 00:18:25.553
well, then add some more salt
or add whatever you want.

00:18:25.553 --> 00:18:27.724
A lot of times what I'll do
is I'll buy a bunch of stuff,

00:18:27.724 --> 00:18:28.736
just start heating things up.

00:18:28.736 --> 00:18:30.780
I'll cut these mushrooms,
I'll saute this together,

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:32.703
I'll put them in a bowl,
I'll cook the steak.

00:18:32.703 --> 00:18:34.751
And then once I'm done I'll
look and see what I have.

00:18:34.751 --> 00:18:35.584
You're like, oh, you know what?

00:18:35.584 --> 00:18:37.527
I can make a boom, boom, boom.

00:18:37.527 --> 00:18:39.519
So sometimes before I'm
really ready to commit

00:18:39.519 --> 00:18:41.912
to a certain kind of dish,

00:18:41.912 --> 00:18:42.912
I just start cooking.

00:18:42.912 --> 00:18:44.878
Get the oven on, get the pans hot.

00:18:44.878 --> 00:18:45.962
Just start cooking away

00:18:45.962 --> 00:18:48.314
and then the magic will start to come.

00:18:48.314 --> 00:18:49.704
And the great things
about this kind of food

00:18:49.704 --> 00:18:51.519
is everything's going to taste good.

00:18:51.519 --> 00:18:54.439
You throw it on a tortilla,
you heat up a tortilla,

00:18:54.439 --> 00:18:56.058
add a little bit of salsa or something,

00:18:56.058 --> 00:18:58.872
and next thing you know
you're eating delicious tacos.

00:18:58.872 --> 00:19:00.777
Alright, so peppers are there.

00:19:00.777 --> 00:19:02.502
And you know, I'm doing this,

00:19:02.502 --> 00:19:04.130
like these fancy sort of flip things.

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:05.130
You don't have to do that,

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:06.488
but if you want to do it,

00:19:06.488 --> 00:19:08.447
just know that it's all in the confidence.

00:19:08.447 --> 00:19:10.552
So I'm going like this, I'm
kind of getting ready for it.

00:19:10.552 --> 00:19:12.573
And you sort of, oops,
see I even lost one,

00:19:12.573 --> 00:19:13.406
but it's no big deal.

00:19:13.406 --> 00:19:15.095
You throw it on the floor, it's delicious.

00:19:15.095 --> 00:19:17.595
So basically pushing forward

00:19:17.595 --> 00:19:19.894
and as I'm pushing, at the right moment

00:19:19.894 --> 00:19:21.391
I just kind of pull back,

00:19:21.391 --> 00:19:22.913
and it's a finesse thing.

00:19:22.913 --> 00:19:24.434
You feel it, you practice it.

00:19:24.434 --> 00:19:26.399
Before you know it, you'll
be impressing your friends,

00:19:26.399 --> 00:19:28.444
and they'll be like,
wow, he can do the thing.

00:19:28.444 --> 00:19:30.090
That's the thing when you're cooking.

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:31.981
The first step to being
impressive and cool

00:19:31.981 --> 00:19:36.064
is being able to do that
thing, that's the thing.

00:19:37.507 --> 00:19:38.578
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:19:38.578 --> 00:19:40.593
I'd love to know what did you think,

00:19:40.593 --> 00:19:42.981
what did you learn from this
that you're going to apply

00:19:42.981 --> 00:19:44.037
somehow to your life.

00:19:44.037 --> 00:19:45.300
I'm really curious.

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:46.133
Are there extra tips

00:19:46.133 --> 00:19:47.774
that you want to add to the list, as well,

00:19:47.774 --> 00:19:49.047
that maybe we missed out?

00:19:49.047 --> 00:19:50.503
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:19:50.503 --> 00:19:51.863
I'm really curious to
see what you have to say.

00:19:51.863 --> 00:19:53.318
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:19:53.318 --> 00:19:54.676
I believe in you.

00:19:54.676 --> 00:19:56.611
Hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:19:56.611 --> 00:19:59.055
and whatever your one word is.

00:19:59.055 --> 00:20:00.594
Much love.

00:20:00.594 --> 00:20:02.094
I'll see you soon.

00:20:08.165 --> 00:20:09.876
- Start clean.

00:20:09.876 --> 00:20:12.486
Start with an empty
dishwasher, an empty sink,

00:20:12.486 --> 00:20:14.431
and clean countertops.

00:20:14.431 --> 00:20:16.725
Cooking will create
dirty dishes and stuff.

00:20:16.725 --> 00:20:19.340
You use spoons and
spatulas and pots and plans

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:22.737
and plates and scoops and potato ricers,

00:20:22.737 --> 00:20:25.131
so if you have a few
areas to put these items

00:20:25.131 --> 00:20:28.448
instead of piling items
on top of other items,

00:20:28.448 --> 00:20:30.213
you're in good.

00:20:30.213 --> 00:20:32.171
Use a garbage bowl.

00:20:32.171 --> 00:20:33.661
Save trips to the garbage can

00:20:33.661 --> 00:20:37.453
by keeping a garbage bowl
close to your working area.

00:20:37.453 --> 00:20:39.960
Prepare your ingredients ahead of time.

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.690
Building your recipe requires
adding certain ingredients

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:45.125
in at the exact right time,

00:20:45.125 --> 00:20:47.015
and of course doing this reduces the mess

00:20:47.015 --> 00:20:49.528
created from that frantic scramble

00:20:49.528 --> 00:20:52.013
to measure the right amount
of paprika or something.

00:20:52.013 --> 00:20:54.398
Get little bowls or a couple of plates

00:20:54.398 --> 00:20:55.231
and do it that way.

00:20:55.231 --> 00:20:58.471
These can be easily washed
when everything's cooking.

00:20:58.471 --> 00:21:00.783
Avoid cross contamination.

00:21:00.783 --> 00:21:03.361
Ensure you have separate
cutting boards and utensils

00:21:03.361 --> 00:21:04.996
for meat and vegetables.

00:21:04.996 --> 00:21:07.917
Clean cloths, keep those handy
for both wiping your hands

00:21:07.917 --> 00:21:09.159
and cleaning up spills,

00:21:09.159 --> 00:21:11.121
and of course, paper towels.

00:21:11.121 --> 00:21:14.206
That way you can use them
for cleaning up meat juices.

00:21:14.206 --> 00:21:16.938
Sometimes I even preportion paper towels

00:21:16.938 --> 00:21:18.318
so that I don't have to touch the roll

00:21:18.318 --> 00:21:20.068
with chicken fingers.

00:21:21.050 --> 00:21:23.328
Clean spills immediately.

00:21:23.328 --> 00:21:25.794
I know it seems obvious,
but if you spill something

00:21:25.794 --> 00:21:28.776
on the floor, the stovetop,
the counter, or the backsplash,

00:21:28.776 --> 00:21:30.757
wipe it up right then and there,

00:21:30.757 --> 00:21:33.482
because that means less
scrubbing for later.

00:21:33.482 --> 00:21:35.118
Use a utensil soak.

00:21:35.118 --> 00:21:38.877
This makes it so you don't
have to even use the dishwasher

00:21:38.877 --> 00:21:40.299
for most of the utensils and cutlery

00:21:40.299 --> 00:21:42.074
that you've used while cooking.

00:21:42.074 --> 00:21:44.333
Just fill a sink or a bowl with hot water

00:21:44.333 --> 00:21:46.376
and a couple of pumps of dish soap.

00:21:46.376 --> 00:21:47.933
When you've got some down time,

00:21:47.933 --> 00:21:49.851
say, while onions are sauteing,

00:21:49.851 --> 00:21:52.161
you can give these a quick rinse and dry.

00:21:52.161 --> 00:21:53.563
This is a great thing to do also

00:21:53.563 --> 00:21:54.981
if you're hosting a party

00:21:54.981 --> 00:21:56.713
and you need to reuse the salad forks

00:21:56.713 --> 00:21:58.768
for dessert forks.

00:21:58.768 --> 00:22:00.381
Splatter screen.

00:22:00.381 --> 00:22:03.365
These things make a huge
difference in oil splatters

00:22:03.365 --> 00:22:05.302
around your stovetop and your backsplash

00:22:05.302 --> 00:22:07.499
and even your air vent filters.

00:22:07.499 --> 00:22:09.635
Wash items as you go.

00:22:09.635 --> 00:22:11.726
When you use a knife, you wash the knife.

00:22:11.726 --> 00:22:14.786
When you use a measuring cup,
you wash the measuring cup.

00:22:14.786 --> 00:22:18.751
To save water, I just spray
dish soap and water on the item,

00:22:18.751 --> 00:22:22.429
scrub it with a sponge, and
then rinse it and let it dry.

00:22:22.429 --> 00:22:25.681
It reduces the strain and the
load size for the dishwasher,

00:22:25.681 --> 00:22:28.631
and it makes cleaning up
after cooking so much easier,

00:22:28.631 --> 00:22:30.573
since you're just putting
items away as you go

00:22:30.573 --> 00:22:32.592
instead of having to put
them in the dishwasher

00:22:32.592 --> 00:22:34.071
and deal with them later.

00:22:34.071 --> 00:22:36.035
Clean while you wait.

00:22:36.035 --> 00:22:39.074
If you're waiting for pasta
to cook or a sauce to reduce,

00:22:39.074 --> 00:22:40.673
you've got a few minutes.

00:22:40.673 --> 00:22:43.741
This is your golden opportunity
to wipe up counters,

00:22:43.741 --> 00:22:45.369
place items in the dishwasher,

00:22:45.369 --> 00:22:48.383
or dry anything that's been hand-washed.

00:22:48.383 --> 00:22:50.547
Soak items while you eat.

00:22:50.547 --> 00:22:52.553
While you sit down to enjoy your creation,

00:22:52.553 --> 00:22:55.256
you should have a
relatively clean kitchen.

00:22:55.256 --> 00:22:57.780
That being said, remove
any food that you've made

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:00.270
from the cooking vessel before eating

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:02.608
and presoak those cooking
vessels while you eat.

00:23:02.608 --> 00:23:03.978
That way, you've got a head start

00:23:03.978 --> 00:23:06.831
on arguably the hardest stuff to clean.

